  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 214 PM EST Sunday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Light snow showers in the Adirondacks, northern \nGreens, and northeastern highlands in Vermont have persisted \ninto early afternoon. Water vapor imagery shows an additional \nshortwave to our north pivoting towards northeastern Vermont \nwhich should keep slightly higher chances of showers in this \nregion into the evening. To the west, decreasing radar returns \nnearer to KTYX show cloud tops have warmed/lowered, and there \nare some increasing breaks in the clouds cover per webcams and \nsatellite imagery. Overall, this suggests further upslope \nprecipitation will trend lighter and more intermittent; expect \nno travel impacts given these precipitation rates and \nincreasingly localized precipitation. Also, while wet roads \noften leads to risk of ice formation at night, persistent \nnorthwest flow and cloud layer will prevent radiative cooling \nthat is needed for more substantial surface temperature falls. \nIn fact, based on recent temperature verification and this \ncloudy and breezy pattern, we went closer to raw model guidance \nand away from bias-corrected and more climatological overnight \ntemperatures. As such, think most lower elevation spots will see\nlow temperatures only bottom out near or slightly above \nfreezing rather than well below freezing.\n\nWhile increasingly anti-cyclonic flow will lead to dry weather \non Monday, low level moisture will be trapped due to strong \nsubsidence. As a result, persistent cloud cover just about\nareawide will be likely through the day as winds remain out of \nthe northwest. This cloudiness will limit mixing depth and \nheating, so it will be one more day with seasonably chilly \ndaytime temperatures in the upper 30s to mid 40s despite rising \ntemperatures aloft. In contrast, on Tuesday as the center of a \nlarge ridge of high pressure to our southwest migrates nearer \nand low level flow becomes southwesterly, we should scatter the \nshallow cloud cover with a mainly sunny sky for most of the day \nanticipated. The combination of a moderating air mass and \nsunshine should lead to the first in a series of days with high \ntemperatures at least 10 degrees above normal.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: A ridge of high pressure will remain entrenched across \nmuch of the eastern CONUS for the latter half of this week. The \nresult will be a continued period of dry weather. A series of \nshortwave troughs/frontal systems will attempt to break down the \nridge late in the week toward next weekend. However, the ridge looks \nto hold firm, shunting northern stream systems to our north, with \nperhaps a glancing blow along the international border on Friday. \nModels have some consensus in better chances for precipitation later \nnext weekend, but there's not much agreement in deterministic or \nensemble guidance in exactly how things would play out. In addition \nto the dry weather, temperatures will remain above normal, \nparticularly during the day when highs in the 50s will be common \n(low to mid 40s is normal for mid November). Lows will be a bit more \nseasonable due to a fairly favorable radiational cooling pattern; \nlows should remain in the 30s to around 40F most nights.",
